% ch-04-ex-03-producer-consumer-acc-reverse.glp
%
% This file presents producer/2 + consumer/3 (duplicated byte-exact from the
% sibling naive-reverse file per FR-010 self-containment) plus the
% accumulator-based reverse/2 + reverse_acc/3 from book p 32, §4.2.4. The
% accumulator implementation runs in O(n) — linear, in contrast to the naive
% O(n²) version in the sibling file.
%
% Per Clarifications Q4 (2026-04-30 spec amendment), ex-03 contains two .glp
% files because both naive reverse/2 (book p 31) and accumulator reverse/2
% (book p 32) define the same predicate name; the GLP non-contiguous-clauses
% constraint forbids them in a single source file. The split mirrors the
% ch02 ex-01 LP/GLP and ch03 ex-01 Program3.1+producer-consumer two-file
% patterns established in earlier chapters.
%
% Programs included (byte-exact):
%   §4.2.1 (book p 31): producer/2 — duplicated inline per FR-010
%   §4.2.2 (book p 31): consumer/3 — duplicated inline per FR-010
%   §4.2.4 (book p 32): accumulator reverse/2 + reverse_acc/3 (O(n) linear)
%
% Cross-chapter inversion: the producer/2 + consumer/3 clauses in this file
% are byte-identical to ch03 ex-01's import (and to the sibling naive-reverse
% file). Per FR-002 + SC-007, the identity contract is preserved.
%
% References Formal 4.2 (SRSW in Continuation Calls, book p 31).

%% §4.2.1 (book p 31): producer that counts down from N. Duplicated per FR-010.

%% Producer base — when count is zero, the stream is the empty list.
producer([], 0).
%% Producer recursive — emit N at head, recurse on tail with N-1.
producer([N?|Xs?], N) :- N? > 0 | N1 := N? - 1, producer(Xs, N1?).

%% §4.2.2 (book p 31): consumer that sums stream elements. Duplicated per FR-010.

%% Consumer base — when stream is empty, accumulator becomes the result.
consumer([], Sum, Sum?).
%% Consumer recursive — `ground(X?)` waits for head to bind; accumulate; recurse via reader.
consumer([X|Xs], Sum, Result?) :- ground(X?) |
    Sum1 := Sum? + X?,
    consumer(Xs?, Sum1?, Result).

%% §4.2.4 (book p 32): accumulator-based reverse/2 + reverse_acc/3.

%% Reverse-with-accumulator entry — initialises Acc to empty list, hands off to reverse_acc/3.
reverse(Xs, Ys?) :- reverse_acc(Xs?, [], Ys).

%% reverse_acc base — when input list is empty, accumulator IS the result via writer/reader pair Acc/Acc?.
reverse_acc([], Acc, Acc?).
%% reverse_acc recursive — prepend head to accumulator, recurse on tail; total work O(n).
reverse_acc([X|Xs], Acc, Ys?) :- reverse_acc(Xs?, [X?|Acc?], Ys).
